@page "/vanilla"

<PageTitle>Vanilla interop</PageTitle>
<h1>Vanilla interop</h1>
<p>A native InputText and ValidationMessage working beside a Formidable input in the same form — the css provider even gives the native input the same state classes.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "VanillaInterop.razor", "VanillaInterop.razor.cs", "GadgetOrder.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Nickname</strong> (a native <code>InputText</code>) and <strong>Colour</strong> (a Formidable input) — both required, validated live, in the same form.</li>
            <li>The css provider gives native InputBase components the same state classes, so both inputs style identically with zero extra wiring.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Submit empty — both inputs show the identical red border.</li>
            <li>Note the native <code>ValidationMessage</code> and Formidable's <code>FieldMessage</code> rendering side by side.</li>
            <li>Fill both and submit — the status line confirms.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm Model="_order" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid">
    <FormSummary />

    <div class="field"><label>Nickname (native InputText) <InputText @bind-Value="_order.Nickname" /></label>
        <ValidationMessage For="() => _order.Nickname" />
        <FieldAnchor For="() => _order.Nickname" /></div>

    <div class="field"><label>Colour (Formidable input) <FormidableInputText For="() => _order.Colour" @bind-Value="_order.Colour" /></label>
        <FieldMessage For="() => _order.Colour" /></div>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>
